abstract: >-
  Weil's explicit formula for ζ(s) equates a functional 𝒜(F), which depends only on the archimedean data of ζ, with
  sums over the zeros and over the prime powers. We construct an explicit even function F = Ξ²H, with H entire and
  positive on ℝ, such that F ≥ 0 and F̂ ≥ 0 on ℝ, F̂ vanishes at log n/(2π) for every integer n ≥ 2, and 𝒜(F) = 0.
  Since Ξ vanishes at every non-trivial zero, on or off the critical line, no information about the zeros is used. F
  is an exact magic function, as in the sphere-packing bounds of Cohn–Elkies and Viazovska, for the linear programme
  of Odlyzko, Poitou and Serre: for the data of ζ the programme has optimal value at most 0, so the method is sharp,
  and under RH F is a minimiser. With a support theorem of Part I, every positive solution of the formula, with
  prime measure beyond log 2/(2π), is then ζ's own. This resolves Conjectures S and U of Part I. Moreover, RH holds
  if and only if this classical programme has optimal value exactly 0. H comes from an eigenvalue-¼ eigenfunction of
  the hyperbolic Laplacian through a contour argument of Viazovska's type, with Green's identity in place of
  Cauchy's theorem; its positivity is partly computer-assisted. We do not prove RH; by these results it is
  equivalent to the existence of such a positive solution.
